// A compile-time `sizeof` is folded from kama's model of the target (KR-94), and i386 is outside it: its ABI puts an
// `int64` or `float64` struct member at 4 where every modelled arch puts it at 8, so `Header` is 12 bytes there and
// 16 elsewhere. A guess would only move the error into the C compiler's assertion, so the fold is refused, naming
// the arch. The run-time `sizeof` is unaffected, and a `comptime assert` falls back to the C compiler. The target
// names its `cc` so no host needs an i386 libc to reach the fold: without one, a host with no zig refuses the target
// before emission, which is the toolchain's answer and not the one under test.
type value Header { public uint32 tag; public float64 stamp; }
comptime usize H = sizeof(Header);
fn uint8 main() { return cast<uint8>(H); }
